<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Würzburg Residence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1720–1744</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Würzburg Residenz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> Residence is the baroque palace of the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Würzburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built mainly between 1720 and 1744 to designs by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balthasar-neumann">Balthasar Neumann</a>. Its grand staircase is covered by a vast ceiling fresco painted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-battista-tiepolo">Giovanni Battista Tiepolo</a> in 1752–1753, and the palace, with its court garden and square, has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wurzburg-residence.jpg" alt="Würzburg Residence" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2004-06-27-Germany-Wuerzburg-Lutz_Marten-Residenz_side_view_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
